T = {
    "862": "6.4. Theoretical Generalization and Scope Conditions",

    "863": "Although this study has presented important theoretical insights by conducting an in-depth analysis of the mechanism through which judgment\u2013decision-making conversion is not initiated following an AI utilization training request, its mode of generalization must be understood at the level of theoretical generalization rather than statistical generalization (Yin, 2009). The purpose of this study was not to derive average tendencies across multiple cases but to structurally elucidate the processual mechanism by which decision-making is not initiated despite the existence of judgment. Accordingly, the Judgment\u2013Decision Stagnation Mechanism presented in this study should be understood not as an explanation confined to a particular case but as an analytical framework that can be reproduced when analogous conditions are satisfied.",

    "864": "The theoretical generalization of this study possesses applicability particularly in the following organizational contexts. First, it is applicable to contexts involving the introduction of any disruptive innovation that unsettles an organization\u2019s existing routines, extending beyond simple AI technology adoption to encompass digital transformation (DX), ESG management adoption, and related initiatives. When confronted with such disruptive innovations, even when an organization preemptively forms a new strategic judgment, the phenomenon of a universal organizational bottleneck\u2014whereby the existing institutional infrastructure for Seizing is absent and conversion is severely delayed\u2014manifests identically. Second, it is applicable to public or bureaucratic organizational contexts in which consensus exists regarding a strategic agenda yet formal decision-making procedures are not initiated. Third, it is applicable to situations in which an issue is deferred over an extended period despite the absence of conflict or explicit opposition among organizational members. In these contexts, the non-activation of judgment\u2013decision-making conversion can be interpreted not as an incidental delay but as the product of a structural mechanism.",

    "865": "However, the mechanism formalized in this study is not universally applicable to all organizational situations. The scope conditions of this study are delimited as follows. First, organizational-level judgment must have actually been formed. In cases where judgment itself is absent, the issue should be interpreted as a problem at the Sensing stage rather than the mechanism proposed by this study. Second, the organization must possess formal decision-making structures and procedures. In organizations where the decision-making system is operated through informal networks, the operational indicators of this study cannot be applied in an identical manner. Third, a self-reinforcing stagnation forms only under circumstances in which processual conditions\u2014such as the absence of scheduling, the non-determination of responsibility attribution, and the non-invocation of procedures\u2014are simultaneously or sequentially satisfied.",

    "866": "In conclusion, the mechanism formalized in this study acquires rigorous explanatory power only under the very specific conditional context in which requisite judgment has been formed yet the formal institutions that would carry it forward remain fundamentally silent. This approach is by no means a macro-level ambition to overhaul the entire framework of Dynamic Capabilities Theory; rather, it is a precision scholarly endeavor that targets exclusively the fatal \u2018processual operational gap\u2019 coiled deep within the Seizing stage. In other words, the theoretical generalization pursued by this study proceeds not by seeking an indiscriminate expansion of universal applicability but by explicitly delimiting the rigorous preconditions under which the mechanism can manifest, thereby thoroughly controlling its logical validity. Such meticulously demarcated analytical boundaries preemptively forestall the error of overstating this study\u2019s explanatory power while simultaneously providing a robust analytical criterion that enables subsequent studies to rigorously verify whether the identified stagnation mechanism is reproduced and varied within diverse organizational ecosystems. Should future comparative studies cross-referencing multiple cases or mixed-methods research designs follow, it is anticipated that the actual operational radius of this conditional mechanism can be empirically demonstrated in its entirety.",

    "867": "6.5. Practical Implications",

    "868": "The most substantial practical implication that this study offers to the practitioner landscape lies in the proposition that the reasons why requisite judgments formed after an AI utilization training request failed to enter the trajectory of formal decision-making must be thoroughly dissected through the lens of \u2018organizational design.\u2019 The conventional approach\u2014which has inertially sought to reduce the impact of AI utilization training requests to the completeness of fragmented training curricula or the technical proficiency of individual participants\u2014should be entirely abandoned. Instead, the task of soberly questioning whether the institutional procedures that would propel a firmly established judgment into a full-fledged decision \u2018actually became operative\u2019 must become the new point of departure for all performance diagnostics. As the multi-layered analysis of this study clearly demonstrates, irrespective of the substantiveness of the training provided on-site or the receptive attitudes of organizational members, if the minimum \u2018institutional initiation conditions\u2019 that would elevate formed judgment into a substantive decision have evaporated, organizational-level performance conversion converges upon the conclusion that it will be structurally mired in indefinite delay.",

    "869": "First, performance evaluation of AI utilization training should be redesigned with the criterion of \u2018whether formal procedures were initiated following judgment\u2019 rather than \u2018learning completion.\u2019 In the case organization, despite the clear existence of both the recognition of necessity for AI utilization training and organizational-level judgment, no structure was established for incorporating said judgment into formal decision-making procedures. This implies that the critical determinant of performance conversion lies not in the training itself but in the existence of mechanisms that channel the judgment formed following an AI utilization training request into the decision-making system.",

    "870": "Second, following an AI utilization training request, an explicit responsible agent for receiving judgments and initiating follow-up procedures must be designated in advance. When no responsible department or agent is designated even after judgment has been formed, the issue in question fails to enter the domain of formal decision-making and remains in a state of non-decision. Accordingly, independent of whether training has been completed, an approach that secures a structure for invoking post-judgment procedures at the organizational design stage is required.",

    "871": "Third, this study underscores the importance of organizational design that clearly establishes the initiation conditions and timing of decision-making procedures. In the case organization, minimum procedural measures\u2014such as agenda-setting, schedule establishment, and deadline assignment\u2014were not undertaken following judgment, resulting in the indefinite deferral of decision-making. This demonstrates that even when an organization does not explicitly reject a decision, decision-making may de facto fail to occur if the conditions for procedure initiation are not established.",

    "872": "Fourth, organizations must design minimum institutional triggers to prevent the state of non-decision following an AI utilization training request. However, to prevent the bureaucratization side effects that would arise from imposing uniform mandatory agenda-setting obligations for all issues, the introduction of a \u2018Conditional Trigger Model\u2019 that differentially applies the intensity of triggers according to the strategic importance and budget scale of each issue is called for. For instance, judgments with enterprise-wide ramifications involving budgets above a certain threshold would be subject to a \u2018Hard Trigger\u2019 that mandates automatic referral to the supreme decision-making body immediately upon formation, whereas departmental-level experimental adoptions or small-scale issues would activate a \u2018Soft Trigger\u2019 that merely monitors progress status at regular intervals. Such a mechanism can function as a structural safeguard preventing judgments from remaining adrift within the organization in a state of stagnation.",

    "873": "Fifth, the minimum unit indicator for monitoring performance conversion following an AI utilization training request should be set not as \u2018the existence of judgment\u2019 but as \u2018whether judgment has been institutionally incorporated.\u2019 The mere fact that judgment has been shared is insufficient; it must be verified that the judgment has actually been included in the procedures whereby it is placed on the formal agenda, a responsible agent is designated, and schedules and responsibilities are assigned. Accordingly, performance evaluation needs to be conducted on the basis of whether post-judgment procedures have been activated rather than on the content of the judgment itself.",

    "874": "In short, the failure of performance conversion following an AI utilization training request cannot be explained solely by the limitations of training programs and must be reexamined as a matter of organizational structural design that enables judgment\u2013decision-making conversion. This perspective transcends the approach of reducing poor performance to individual responsibility and redirects practical strategy toward improving decision-making procedures and structural conditions.",

    "875": "6.6. Limitations of the Study",

    "876": "Although this study provides important implications for elucidating the mechanism through which conversion to organizational performance fails following an AI utilization training request, it possesses several limitations. These limitations are presented not to undermine the conclusions of this study but to clarify the interpretive scope of the research findings and to provide a point of departure for subsequent research.",

    "877": "First, this study is limited in its generalizability in that it is grounded in a qualitative single-case study. The purpose of this study was not to derive general causal relationships by comparing multiple cases but to conduct an in-depth examination of the processual mechanism through which judgment\u2013decision-making conversion is not initiated following an AI utilization training request. Accordingly, the findings of this study should be understood from the perspective of theoretical generalization rather than statistical generalization, and cautious interpretation is required when applying them to other organizational contexts (Yin, 2009).",

    "878": "In particular, because this study takes as its object of analysis the phenomenon in which decision-making did not actually occur following judgment\u2014that is, the phenomena of non-decision and non-event\u2014it was determined that a micro-level, process-centered analysis of a single case was theoretically more appropriate than a multi-case comparative design in which variables and outcomes are determined ex ante (Eisenhardt, 1989; Bennett & Checkel, 2015). While a multi-case design may be effective for comparing outcomes centering on decisions that were actually made, it possesses analytical limitations for research questions such as those of the present study, which seek to explain \u2018the reasons why a decision did not occur\u2019 and \u2018the state in which a process was not initiated.\u2019",

    "879": "Second, this study possesses the limitation of having been conducted on the premise of the positionality of an insider researcher. The researcher, as an internal member of the case organization, was positioned to access the organization\u2019s administrative and operational processes; however, the researcher did not occupy a position with direct authority over judgment formation or decision-making initiation following the AI utilization training request. In other words, the researcher occupied the position of an observer with respect to the issue in question and did not possess the structural authority to influence whether decision-making was initiated or its outcomes.",

    "880": "Such positionality simultaneously entails the depth of data access and the potential for interpretive bias. This study sought to secure the reliability of interpretation by mitigating this limitation through data triangulation, process tracing, and the use of data centered on official documents and system records; however, the possibility that the researcher\u2019s positionality influenced the analysis cannot be entirely excluded.",

    "881": "Third, in that this study analyzed a case in which judgment\u2013decision-making conversion did not occur, it did not include a comparative analysis with cases in which decisions were actually made. Consequently, rather than directly verifying the conditions under which judgment\u2013decision-making conversion was successfully achieved, the analysis was developed centering on the conditions under which conversion was interrupted or the state of non-initiation was maintained.",

    "882": "However, this represents not a design deficiency but a consequence of the theoretical choice to establish non-decision as an independent object of analysis. Non-decision has been understood not merely as \u2018a result in which a decision was not made\u2019 but as a phenomenon that is formed and sustained through the combination of structural and processual conditions that prevent the decision-making procedure itself from being initiated (Bachrach & Baratz, 1962). Such phenomena can be adequately analyzed only through process tracing methods that reconstruct how the process was blocked and deferred over the course of time, rather than through event-centered comparative analysis (Bennett & Checkel, 2015).",

    "883": "Fourth, this study delimited its analysis to the specific organizational change context of AI utilization training requests. Although the processual mechanism derived from this study possesses applicability to other organizational change contexts similar to digital transformation or technology adoption, additional verification is required regarding whether it can be identically applied to contexts of organizational learning or strategic execution involving types other than AI training. That is, the explanatory power of this study is delimited to the conditional situation of \u2018an organizational context in which judgment exists yet decision-making is not initiated.\u2019",

    "884": "Fifth, the substantive period during which the case data of this study were generated spans approximately six to seven weeks (Appendix A: December 24, 2025 \u2013 Appendix D: February 9, 2026). When compared with the organization\u2019s typical agenda processing cycles (e.g., quarterly personnel committee meetings, budget review schedules), this period may not represent a sufficient passage of time. Accordingly, it is difficult to definitively determine from the data of this study alone whether the observed state of non-decision constitutes \u2018structural entrenchment\u2019 or falls within the category of \u2018normal processing delay.\u2019 To compensate for this limitation, comparison with the organization\u2019s average agenda processing period or longer-term follow-up observation would be necessary.",

    "885": "In short, the limitations of this study derive from research design and analytical scope and need to be understood as structurally separate from the core contribution of this study\u2014namely, the elucidation of the processual mechanism of judgment\u2013decision-making conversion. Rather, by establishing \u2018the state in which a decision does not occur\u2019\u2014which had been relatively overlooked in Dynamic Capabilities Theory and organizational decision-making research\u2014as an explicable object of analysis, this study provides a theoretical and methodological foundation that can be extended through multi-case comparison or mixed-methods research design in subsequent studies.",

    "886": "Such transparent disclosure of limitations originates by no means from an intention to constrain the explanatory radius of this study. Rather, it should be interpreted as an essential act of \u2018conceptual boundary setting\u2019\u2014delineating the specific contexts under which the newly formalized \u2018Judgment\u2013Decision Stagnation Mechanism\u2019 can fully operate.",

    "887": "6.7. Summary",

    "888": "Chapter 6 transcends the dimension of mechanically recapitulating the empirical analysis and theoretical argumentation developed in the preceding chapters. It functions as the pivotal \u2018integrative chapter\u2019 that fully reassembles the critical judgment\u2013decision conversion stagnation phenomenon observed immediately after the on-site AI utilization training request was raised, at a single highly refined mechanism stratum. The fundamental orientation of this chapter lies not in tautologically enumerating the post hoc result of \u2018the absence of visible performance outcomes.\u2019 Rather, it lies in meticulously elucidating through a structural lens the profound disjuncture whereby, despite firm judgment having been established within the organization, that judgment fails to ascend to the stage of substantive decision-making.",

    "889": "Tracing the trajectory of its multi-layered case analysis, this study has unequivocally confirmed that the mere fact of requisite judgment firmly existing within a macro-systemic framework does not automatically generate formal decision-making. In other words, it repeatedly declares that the root cause of the failure of conversion to decision-making cannot be reduced to the completeness of fragmented training curricula or the technical proficiency of individual practitioners. Rather, it converges upon the firm conclusion that the structural blind spot must be identified in the state in which the \u2018initiation conditions\u2019 of the procedures that would propel the formed judgment into a complete decision have been fundamentally paralyzed. This innovative perspective thoroughly dismantles the shallow success-versus-failure dichotomy that had pervaded the existing academic discourse with its performance-indicator-oriented yardstick, and proposes an entirely new multi-dimensional explanatory framework to the scholarly community.",

    "890": "Above all, this chapter has perfectly demonstrated\u2014taking as its core reference point the \u2018Judgment\u2013Decision Stagnation Mechanism\u2019 firmly formalized through the preceding Chapter 5\u2014that the non-decision phase observed on-site is not the product of transient administrative delay. Rather, it has once again structurally synthesized that this constitutes a \u2018self-reinforcing loop\u2019 in which four critical deficiencies\u2014the failure of incorporation into the organization\u2019s formal temporal framework, the ambiguity of responsibility attribution, the non-activation of procedural thresholds, and the concealment of stagnation itself\u2014mesh like interlocking gears and amplify vigorously.",

    "891": "Finally, this chapter has opened a new horizon of theoretical extension by reconstructing the macro-level framework of Dynamic Capabilities Theory through a thoroughly processual perspective. Boldly departing from the inertia that had treated the Seizing stage as the post hoc moment at which a strategic determination was made, it was comprehensively repositioned as a \u2018processual interval\u2019 that awakens only when rigorous prerequisites are fully conjoined. Through this, a weighty scholarly contribution was achieved by elevating the terrain of persistent stagnation and disjuncture\u2014which the prevailing paradigm had never captured\u2014into the very center of the theory.",

    "892": "Through this, Seizing was repositioned as a stage defined not by \u2018the moment of decision\u2019 but by \u2018whether the pathway through which judgment enters the formal decision-making structure was actually opened.\u2019",

    "893": "Additionally, this chapter reorganized the practical implications for organizational design following an AI utilization training request. It emphasized that the existence of judgment alone cannot explain performance conversion and that conversion can be actualized only when minimum structural conditions\u2014such as the designation of a decision-making agent, agenda-setting procedures, and the establishment of schedules and responsibilities\u2014are put in place. This shifts the unit of analysis from reducing the success or failure of AI training to a matter of individual learning to addressing the problem of organizational design that enables judgment\u2013decision-making conversion.",

    "894": "In conclusion, this chapter transparently reflected upon the methodological limitations inherent in the research design. Although the approach of focusing on a single case and the unique positionality of an insider researcher do constrain the radius of generalization at the macro level to a certain degree, these very features conversely served as the most powerful methodological foundation enabling unprecedentedly incisive and precise tracing of the micro-level operational trajectory from judgment to decision. Such explicit disclosure of limitations is by no means intended to diminish the scholarly accomplishments achieved by this study; rather, it should be assessed as an essential work of \u2018theoretical boundary setting\u2019 that demarcates the rigorous preconditions under which the newly derived \u2018Judgment\u2013Decision Stagnation Mechanism\u2019 can fully operate.",

    "895": "In comprehensive overview, Chapter 6 constitutes the core section that has confirmed\u2014at the stratum of a highly refined mechanism\u2014the central thesis that \u2018the mere existence of judgment within a macro-systemic framework by no means mechanically guarantees the generation of substantive decision-making.\u2019 Furthermore, it perfectly fulfills its role as the robust \u2018integrative chapter\u2019 that fuses these empirical findings into the theoretical extension of the existing paradigm and the practical renewal of the field.",

    "896": "On the basis of the logical argumentation accumulated thus far, the following chapter develops in depth how the empirical findings of this study can contribute both scholarly and practically to subsequent research in the academic community and to the practitioner landscape. The concluding Chapter 7, taking as its firm foundation the processual insights unearthed by this study, presents in a multi-dimensional manner the macro-level theoretical directions that will guide the evolution of Dynamic Capabilities Theory, practical recommendations urging a comprehensive overhaul of organizational operations and policy design, and future research agendas that will further expand the scholarly horizon\u2014thereby bringing this dissertation\u2019s extensive journey to its conclusion.",
}

